<Layout title="Zim Zoom Case Study — Afterglow Automations" description="How Zim Zoom turned scattered event operations into one owner-focused command center.">
  <article class="study"><a class="back" href="/#work">← Back to the work</a><header><p class="eyebrow">CASE STUDY 01 · PHOTO BOOTH OPERATIONS</p><h1>From scattered data to one <i>command center.</i></h1><p>How Zim Zoom connected events, capacity, staffing, payments, metrics, and approvals into one owner-focused operating view—in a matter of days.</p></header><img class="study-hero pink-frame" src="/images/zimzoom-dashboard.png" alt="Zim Zoom owner overview"/><div class="study-body"><aside><span>CLIENT</span><b>Zim Zoom Photo Booth</b><span>FOCUS</span><b>Operations + AI systems</b><span>MY ROLE</span><b>Strategy, product, UX, engineering</b></aside><div><h2>The owner had become the integration layer.</h2><p>VSCO held customers, jobs, orders, and payments. Superhuman Docs held metrics. Slack held approvals. Staffing and booth capacity depended on manual interpretation. Each tool worked; the daily burden came from connecting them.</p><blockquote>Before, the owner had to search for problems. Now, the command center brings the right problems to the owner.</blockquote><h2>One screen. Six essential answers.</h2><ol><li>Revenue booked and collected</li><li>Dates nearing booth capacity</li><li>Upcoming shifts still unstaffed</li><li>Customers with past-due balances</li><li>Decisions waiting on the owner</li><li>Connected-system health</li></ol><h2>The projected return</h2><p>The initial operating model estimates 7–16 hours saved per week—roughly 350–800 hours per year. Those are modeled estimates to be replaced with measured results after enough production use.</p></div></div><div class="study-gallery"><img src="/images/zimzoom-capacity.png" alt="Booth capacity grid"/><img src="/images/zimzoom-staffing.png" alt="Staffing recommendations"/></div><a class="next-study" href="/work/southern-revelry">Next: Southern Revelry <span>↗</span></a></article>
</Layout>
